{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 2,
  "simulation_id": "sim-05",
  "member_id": "pearce-mark",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B014",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B032",
    "B022"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged: I favor B014 for Katori and Ye. The magic-wavelength lattice-clock idea and its decisive experimental development form a bounded physical achievement, not simply an incremental improvement in timekeeping. It has passed the demanding test that matters most for an instrument-led discovery: independent realization with quantified systematic control, followed by durable use as a precision-measurement platform for timekeeping, relativistic geodesy, and fundamental tests. The proposed pair credibly spans the central conceptual advance and defining realization, provided the citation is confined to optical lattice clocks rather than the whole optical-clock enterprise.",
    "case_against": "The principal remaining reservation is historical attribution. Optical-frequency metrology and the subsequent development of operational clock systems involved a wide community, and a two-person award can be justified only for the specifically delimited magic-wavelength lattice-clock achievement. The citation should not borrow force from a prospective redefinition of the second; the demonstrated measurement capability is the relevant mature result.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig favors B032 because Jarzynski, Crooks, and Seifert supplied a durable general framework for irreversibility, while acknowledging uncertainty about its experimental prominence and the boundary among related theorems.",
        "response": "I agree that B032 is a profound and coherent theoretical alternative, and it is now one of my two retained alternatives. But its own need to delimit several related relations and a later framework makes the prize object less singular. B014 combines a comparably clear physical principle with repeatedly demonstrated, independently reproduced experimental performance. On maturity and exact credit, I therefore still judge the lattice-clock case readier."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson identifies B022 as a fully mature and consequential materials invention, while warning that its boundary must be separated from prior rare-earth-magnet work, later optimization, and manufacturing development.",
        "response": "I share the assessment that B022 is an outstanding, durable alternative and retain it for that reason. Its practical importance is not in doubt. Yet the archival boundary identified by Eriksson is decisive for an individual award: parallel invention, antecedent materials work, and later alloy development must be distinguished before the proposed two-name attribution can be treated as comparably secure. B014 presently has the cleaner chain from physical solution to defining realization."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll stresses that the lattice-clock achievement should be limited to the magic-wavelength concept and decisive realization, rather than credited as the entire optical-clock revolution.",
        "response": "I agree. This qualification resolves the strongest concern without diminishing the achievement: the award would recognize the solution that allowed many confined atoms to be used without sacrificing clock precision, and the experimental work that made it a robust platform. It should not claim credit for all optical-frequency metrology or for later institutional standards decisions."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "A final historical priority review should confirm that Katori and Ye alone adequately represent the conception and decisive realization of optical lattice clocks, while documenting the boundary to other optical-clock pioneers.",
      "For B032, further assessment is needed of whether the linked fluctuation relations and stochastic-thermodynamic framework constitute one sufficiently bounded prize achievement relative to their experimental validation.",
      "For B022, archival review remains necessary to separate the original inventions from prior rare-earth-magnet development, subsequent alloy optimization, and manufacturing contributions."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
